WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.293
This is not meant to be sensational.

00:00:02.293 --> 00:00:04.754
There's no need for us to
sensationalize any data.

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:07.090
But the truth of the matter is for
the last two to three years now,

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.260
guns have been the leading
cause of death among kids.

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:15.974
Physicians are treaters of the sick
and preservers of the healthy.

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:18.935
So, there really is nothing else
that a group of physicians

00:00:18.935 --> 00:00:21.813
would rally behind
other than saving lives.

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:24.315
This epidemic is uniquely American.

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:27.027
Gun violence is a public health
issue and a health-care issue.

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:29.779
This is not Democratic or Republican.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:32.490
This is just protecting humans.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.826
Sometimes you have to lose.

00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:37.120
Sometimes you have
to push a boulder up

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.872
a hill and it has to crash
back down on you.

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:40.832
But you won't succeed
without ever trying.

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:51.634
America supports all these bills.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.846
And it needs to be the time now
where the politicians realize

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:58.099
that if government doesn't back us,
that they're going to be voted out.

00:00:58.099 --> 00:00:59.100
They won’t be politicians anymore.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:00:59.600
Exactly.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.023
We have seen that when we take public
health approaches, that we save lives.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.611
Motor vehicle accidents are no longer

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:11.196
the number one cause
of death of children

00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:15.200
because of safer cars and car seats.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.703
We can use that same
public health approach

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:22.707
to discuss guns and
gun safety legislation

00:01:22.707 --> 00:01:24.959
and create meaningful change.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.921
How can we as medical experts,

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:29.089
help you guys?

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:29.798
So what do you need?

00:01:29.798 --> 00:01:31.174
Do you need more doctors

00:01:31.174 --> 00:01:34.427
from your state that we can help
provide you to provide evidence?

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.429
Do you need us to come
to hearings for you?

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.265
We are here to collaborate with
your office to whip these votes

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.560
because we are sick of letting
children die every day.

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:46.689
A typical day is going
from meeting to meeting.

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:47.565
No breaks.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:53.530
Lobbying all day and presenting
our statistics and data and research.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.909
I usually try to open and say,
my name is Dr. Emily Lieberman.

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:01.454
I'm a pediatrician, a wife, a mother.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:04.207
I am now also a mass shooting survivor.

00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:07.919
And I have the unfortunate but unique

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:13.508
perspective of now being a survivor
of gun violence and also a pediatrician

00:02:13.508 --> 00:02:18.805
who treats people in the community
who have suffered from gun violence.

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:23.685
And I think that sets us apart
from a lot of the other meetings.

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:29.566
We live in Highland Park,
which prior to July 4th of last year,

00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:30.733
I think anyone would say

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:34.571
was probably one of the safest
appearing communities in the country.

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:37.407
We're kind of Pleasantville
for lack of a better word.

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:39.826
On our nation's most patriotic day,

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:42.996
July 4th, our family woke up,

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:46.040
put on 4th of July attire

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.459
and met with cousins and my parents.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:50.420
Our children were in the streets.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:54.132
There was laughter.
We’re waving to politicians.

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:59.220
And then all of a sudden there is
a horrible popping sound overhead.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:06.644
And it becomes clear very
quickly that this is a gun.

00:03:08.479 --> 00:03:11.816
Seven people were killed and
more than 48 others were injured

00:03:11.816 --> 00:03:16.237
after a gunman opened fire at a 4th
of July parade in Highland Park, Illinois.

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:18.990
According to the Gun Violence Archive,

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:24.954
it was one of 647 mass shootings
that took place in 2022.

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:29.083
Unfortunately, we've learned
that we can't keep our children safe

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:32.295
when most of these weapons
are on the streets.

00:03:32.295 --> 00:03:35.131
But what we can do is help
prevent these shootings

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:36.674
from continuing to occur,

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:39.636
which is what we are here to do now

00:03:39.636 --> 00:03:41.596
for the rest of our life.

00:03:46.517 --> 00:03:49.062
This is a pediatric surgery OR.

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:52.523
And so typically when a patient rolls
in, they're going to be lying down here.

00:03:52.523 --> 00:03:54.025
This is where the anesthesiologist is.

00:03:54.025 --> 00:03:57.445
And then once the patient is asleep,
the whole team kind of functions

00:03:57.445 --> 00:03:59.864
very much as a well-oiled machine.

00:03:59.864 --> 00:04:05.328
We've here on the front lines have
seen, have felt that 350% increase

00:04:05.328 --> 00:04:09.832
in the number of kids with gun
injuries last year, here in New York.

00:04:09.832 --> 00:04:12.752
The teams are getting
increasingly fatigued.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:16.047
The trauma from having to deal with
not only children who have gun injuries,

00:04:16.047 --> 00:04:17.173
but their families.

00:04:17.173 --> 00:04:20.218
And constantly telling families
that they've either lost a loved one

00:04:20.218 --> 00:04:24.180
to a preventable disease or that their kid
is now disabled for life or brain dead.

00:04:24.847 --> 00:04:28.059
In little children, all the
organs are closer together.

00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:32.105
And as a result, every bullet can
injure that many more organs.

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:37.610
And is that much more devastating than,
let's say, a bullet injury in a adult.

00:04:38.695 --> 00:04:41.656
We have a universal screening program
here in our emergency departments

00:04:41.656 --> 00:04:45.618
where we actually screen
all patients for firearm injury risk.

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:49.163
And if you screen positive for that,
there are resources we can give you

00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:52.667
that help break that cycle of violence
before the gunshot even happens.

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:54.961
Good morning.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:59.674
AAP guidelines suggest that the best
advice is to not have a gun in the home.

00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:02.677
But for those gun owners
that actually do have guns,

00:05:02.677 --> 00:05:06.764
research shows that only 50%
of them are safely storing it.

00:05:06.764 --> 00:05:10.935
So something that we give out to
our patients are these cable locks.

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:13.104
This will go through the
unloaded, empty firearm.

00:05:13.104 --> 00:05:14.647
It goes in one spot there.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:15.940
You'll lock it.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:17.817
The key comes out.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:18.985
And you have a safe firearm.

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:22.405
When we talk about gun violence,
we are referring to firearm-related

00:05:22.405 --> 00:05:26.367
suicides, unintentional injuries,
otherwise known as accidental injuries.

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:28.619
We're talking about
intentional firearm injuries,

00:05:28.619 --> 00:05:31.873
which could be firearm violence/assault

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:36.044
or in a small sliver of cases,
things like public mass shootings.

00:05:36.044 --> 00:05:40.048
And it's really important that we
start having this discussion in a very

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:41.758
nuanced, granular way

00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:43.217
because that is the only way

00:05:43.217 --> 00:05:46.304
that we're going to have a productive
conversation around potential solutions.

00:05:46.304 --> 00:05:49.349
You can imagine that the
solutions to firearm suicide

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:52.518
are far different than the solutions
needed to stop mass shootings,

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:56.314
which are far different than the solutions
that are needed for firearm violence.

00:05:56.314 --> 00:05:57.648
We cannot lump it all together.

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:03.613
I've lived in the same
house my whole life and

00:06:03.613 --> 00:06:05.615
I don't see myself
moving anytime soon.

00:06:05.615 --> 00:06:07.533
We're here to stay. City kid.

00:06:07.992 --> 00:06:10.703
Growing up in this neighborhood,
gun violence, it’s always been prevalent.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:12.163
Always something I've seen around me.

00:06:12.163 --> 00:06:15.583
It just never dawned on me
that it could affect me in a way

00:06:15.583 --> 00:06:17.126
that would change my life.

00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:21.047
The night of the shooting,
we had just went on a family outing.

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:23.383
Came back home. Everyone was in bed.

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:25.635
I decided to go back
outside for a charger

00:06:25.635 --> 00:06:27.512
and I was sitting in the
passenger seat of the car.

00:06:27.512 --> 00:06:29.555
And all of a sudden someone
runs up to the driver's side.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:33.017
They said something, waved some
hand symbols, and that was it.

00:06:33.017 --> 00:06:34.185
They just started unloading.

00:06:35.311 --> 00:06:37.814
When we got to the hospital,
the only injury was

00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:39.440
gunshot wound to the back.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.319
I had a nicked vertebrae,
a severed spinal cord,

00:06:43.319 --> 00:06:47.115
and both my lungs were ruptured,
internal bleeding, the whole nine yards.

00:06:47.115 --> 00:06:48.825
I still have the bullet in my chest today.

00:06:49.367 --> 00:06:51.077
I was in the hospital for about six weeks.

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:52.829
I was on my mother's insurance.

00:06:52.829 --> 00:06:54.163
It was an HMO.

00:06:54.163 --> 00:06:55.248
So we got lucky.

00:06:55.248 --> 00:06:57.375
We didn't have to pay for the visit.

00:06:57.375 --> 00:07:00.461
But the visit itself was
north of 00,000.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.047
When I left the hospital,
I thought the hardest

00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:06.300
part of the journey was done,
but that was not true.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.094
When I got home, I realized

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:11.264
I got steps in the front and the back,
and now I can't use my legs.

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:12.849
How am I going to get up there?

00:07:12.849 --> 00:07:16.978
While insurance providers tend to cover
the majority of medical bills immediately

00:07:16.978 --> 00:07:21.858
following a shooting, survivors
say costs continue to rack up over time.

00:07:21.858 --> 00:07:26.028
Physical therapy or home renovations
may not be covered by insurance

00:07:26.028 --> 00:07:29.657
and finding programs to help cover
the costs can be a challenge.

00:07:29.657 --> 00:07:33.161
As a result, many patients have
turned to crowdsourcing to fundraise

00:07:33.161 --> 00:07:34.662
for ongoing care needs.

00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:37.457
There's a lot of things that have
had to come out-of-pocket

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:40.251
because there are just niche enough
for the insurance not to cover them.

00:07:40.251 --> 00:07:44.088
But important enough that you
actually need them with a disability.

00:07:44.088 --> 00:07:49.218
So, I would say roughly maybe
$20,000 to $30,000 total,

00:07:49.218 --> 00:07:51.721
as of now, has been paid out of pocket.

00:07:54.265 --> 00:07:58.269
Treating gunshot wounds and
patients is extremely expensive.

00:07:58.269 --> 00:08:00.938
We know that treating a patient
with gun injuries, for example,

00:08:00.938 --> 00:08:04.233
is three times more expensive than
treating any other type of trauma.

00:08:04.233 --> 00:08:05.276
This really speaks

00:08:05.276 --> 00:08:08.779
to the complexities of the wounds,
the complications that result from it,

00:08:08.779 --> 00:08:11.199
and the long, ongoing disabilities

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:14.202
that really require a lot of
intense medical management.

00:08:14.202 --> 00:08:16.787
So here we're seeing that gun
violence costs the United States

00:08:16.787 --> 00:08:20.124
over $550 billion per year,

00:08:20.124 --> 00:08:22.502
which is an extraordinary amount.

00:08:22.502 --> 00:08:26.589
Our politicians who enabled
this assault weapons ban in 1994

00:08:26.589 --> 00:08:28.299
truly were trailblazers at the time.

00:08:28.299 --> 00:08:31.677
And sadly, the clause was written
that it would sundown in ten years.

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:35.973
So from my perspective,
we're asking Washington, DC

00:08:35.973 --> 00:08:39.435
to reinitiate a law that was a law.

00:08:39.435 --> 00:08:41.437
We're not rewriting the
history books here.

00:08:41.437 --> 00:08:45.775
We are asking them to
continue the history books.

00:08:47.735 --> 00:08:49.946
The rates of gun violence
are only going up.

00:08:49.946 --> 00:08:53.741
As we delved deeper and saw
how much of a problem this is,

00:08:53.741 --> 00:08:56.536
these underserved communities,
the health disparities.

00:08:56.536 --> 00:08:59.956
Specifically, Brown and Black communities having

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:01.999
horrific day-to-day gun violence.

00:09:01.999 --> 00:09:06.671
And losing many more Brown and
Black people to gun violence.

00:09:06.671 --> 00:09:08.047
We realized that we have to really

00:09:08.047 --> 00:09:10.258
broaden, as physicians,
some of our lobbying.

00:09:11.133 --> 00:09:15.221
We do know that gun-safety policies,
such as assault weapon bans,

00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:19.141
background checks, waiting
periods, licensing requirements

00:09:19.141 --> 00:09:22.603
that most gun owners actually
do support, will save lives.

00:09:22.603 --> 00:09:24.313
And then we know that other policies,

00:09:24.313 --> 00:09:28.067
for example, that address the root
causes of structural racism, inequity

00:09:28.067 --> 00:09:32.863
will improve firearm violence
in inner-city communities.

00:09:32.863 --> 00:09:35.533
So my question really always is:

00:09:35.533 --> 00:09:38.536
how can we have that
conversation in a way

00:09:38.536 --> 00:09:42.456
that legitimizes both
sides and their concerns

00:09:42.456 --> 00:09:45.626
that can really lead to
productive solutions rather than

00:09:45.626 --> 00:09:48.087
just this, them-versus-us discussion.

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:51.716
I’m going probably, probably,

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:52.842
I’m probably going to win.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.220
Uno!

00:09:56.220 --> 00:10:01.392
If this is the public health crisis
that we believe it is, then

00:10:01.392 --> 00:10:04.854
gun violence prevention is going to
be a priority in the next election.

00:10:04.854 --> 00:10:07.898
And these lawmakers need to be held
accountable for their decisions.

00:10:09.275 --> 00:10:12.194
Childhood trauma and violence

00:10:12.194 --> 00:10:14.780
perpetuates childhood
trauma and violence.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.449
And so in this country,

00:10:16.449 --> 00:10:20.494
as we continue to have
more and more gun violence

00:10:20.494 --> 00:10:22.830
and children who are
witnessing these horrific events,

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.999
how it's affecting their family.

00:10:24.999 --> 00:10:28.753
They're actually more likely
to become violent themselves.

00:10:28.753 --> 00:10:33.007
We're trying to change the narrative
in our house to: something terrible

00:10:33.007 --> 00:10:37.595
happened and our parents did
everything possible to try to protect us.

00:10:37.595 --> 00:10:41.974
And teach them that despite
adversity that might happen,

00:10:41.974 --> 00:10:46.354
we will always try to rise above it
and find solutions to problems.

00:10:47.104 --> 00:10:48.314
But our question is
back to the lawmakers.

00:10:48.314 --> 00:10:51.067
Have they done everything
in their power to save lives?

